<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladimir the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 958–1015</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir I of Kiev, Volodymyr the Great, Saint Vladimir · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladimir the Great was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from about 980 to 1015. In 988 he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and married the emperor's sister Anna, bringing churches, Church Slavonic writing and Byzantine art to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. He is venerated as a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great.jpg" alt="Vladimir the Great" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Влад Федченко · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Vladimir_the_Great.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vladimir was the illegitimate youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> by his housekeeper Malusha, and her brother Dobrynya was his tutor and most trusted adviser. Sviatoslav made him ruler of Novgorod in 970 and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to his legitimate son Yaropolk. After Sviatoslav was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> in 972, Yaropolk's war against his brother Oleg in 977 sent Vladimir abroad, where he gathered a Varangian army. He returned, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> by force after its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogvolod">Rogvolod</a> refused him his daughter Rogneda, and slew Yaropolk by treachery to become prince of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Vladimir widened his father's domain: he seized the Cherven towns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 981, suppressed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyatichians">Vyatichi</a> rebellion, subdued the Yatvingians in 983, conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radimichians">Radimichs</a> in 984 and campaigned against the Volga Bulgars in 985. He remained a pagan and raised a temple on a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to six gods, among them Perun, Stribog, Dazhd'bog and Mokosh. A mob killed the Christian Fyodor and his son Ioann, whom later tradition counted as the first Christian martyrs in Rus'.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, missionaries and then envoys examined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, Latin Christianity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and Byzantine Christianity, and those sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> were won over by its worship. In 988, after taking Chersonesus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, he sought the hand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>'s sister Anna and was baptised there with the name Basil. Arab sources tell it differently: the emperor, facing a revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-skleros">Bardas Sclerus</a> and Bardas Phocas, asked for help, and Vladimir sent 6,000 troops in exchange for a marriage and his own conversion.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> he destroyed pagan monuments and built churches, among them the Church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">Tithes</a> in 989. He set his twelve sons over the subject principalities, founded schools, introduced ecclesiastical courts and, according to the chronicles, abolished capital punishment. In 1014 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> stopped paying tribute; Vladimir began gathering troops but fell ill and died at Berestove, near Kiev, in 1015.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches canonised him, and his feast day is 15 July (28 July in the older calendar). Byzantine sources barely mention his baptism, and some historians conclude that the canonical story took shape in the 1040s, when a Rus' church equal to the Byzantine one was wanted. Folk ballads call him Krasno Solnyshko, the Fair Sun. The town of Volodymyr in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> was founded by him, and St Volodymyr's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> is dedicated to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vladimir the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vladimir the Great</b> built lines of earthworks and forts south of Kiev against the Pechenegs and sent about 6,000 warriors to help Basil II in 988, the origin of the Byzantine Varangian Guard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As the second city of Kievan Rus’, Novgorod was usually governed by a son of the grand prince of Kiev: <b>Vladimir the Great</b> and Yaroslav the Wise both ruled there before taking the throne in Kiev, and the stone Cathedral of Saint Sophia was built in 1045–1050.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The fortunes of the war swung under <b>Vladimir I of Kiev</b>, who founded the town of Pereyaslav on the site of his victory over them, and Yaroslav I the Wise defeated them in 1036.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Russian Primary Chronicle records that <b>Vladimir of Kiev</b> campaigned against the Bulgars in 985 and then made peace with them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to legend, when <b>Vladimir the Great</b> sought a new faith for Kievan Rus’, his envoys found “no joy … but sorrow and great stench” in Volga Bulgaria, but were won over by the beauty of the rites in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vladimir</b> of Kiev tried both courses: in 980 he raised idols of Perun and other Slavic gods on a hill above the city, and in 988 he was baptised with his subjects and had the idols burned or thrown into the Dnieper.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Berestove, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.44,30.55&amp;year=995" aria-label="Berestove on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Vladimir the Great"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5030 5814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5030 5814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.44,30.55&amp;year=995">What was here in 995 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 942–972 CE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/rogneda-of-polotsk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/rogneda-of-polotsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Rogneda of Polotsk</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; c. 960 – c. 1000</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaroslav-the-wise.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Yaroslav the Wise</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 978–1054</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatopolk-i-of-kiev"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Sviatopolk I of Kiev</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 980–1019</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/izyaslav-of-polotsk"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Izyaslav of Polotsk</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; d. 1001</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
